WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
Here's a herd of a hundred Shorthorn cattle.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.480
Sixty-four are red, thirty-two are roan, and four

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.200
are white.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.480
From the co-dominance video, you know each roan

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.280
carries one red allele and one white allele.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.760
So what fraction of all the coat colour

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.680
alleles in this herd are white?

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.200
Four per cent?

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.320
Thirty-six per cent?

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.080
Hold that thought, because the answer is neither.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.300
Start with the cattle themselves.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.100
A frequency tells you how common something is

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.780
in the group you counted.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.580
It's the count divided by the total.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.300
Sixty-four out of a hundred cattle are red,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.780
so the frequency of red coats is sixty-four

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:40.900
per cent.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.700
Roan is thirty-two per cent, and white is

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:44.580
four.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.660
A table keeps the counts, and a bar

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.100
chart shows the pattern at a glance.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.860
But always say what you divided by.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.140
Here, it's cattle.

00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:55.486
Now the alleles.

00:00:56.047 --> 00:00:58.446
Every animal carries two copies of the coat

00:00:58.527 --> 00:01:01.646
colour gene, so a hundred cattle hold two

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:02.606
hundred copies.

00:01:03.247 --> 00:01:05.886
Each white cow has two white alleles, which

00:01:06.007 --> 00:01:06.686
makes eight.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:10.046
Each roan has one, which makes thirty-two more.

00:01:10.607 --> 00:01:13.486
That's forty white alleles out of two hundred,

00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:15.726
so the white allele frequency is twenty per

00:01:15.847 --> 00:01:18.766
cent, and the red allele is eighty.

00:01:18.847 --> 00:01:21.246
Four per cent counted only the white cattle,

00:01:21.327 --> 00:01:24.526
and thirty-six counted animals, not alleles.

00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:28.853
Cattle make this easy, because every genotype looks

00:01:28.934 --> 00:01:29.413
different.

00:01:29.894 --> 00:01:30.853
Peas don't.

00:01:31.414 --> 00:01:33.493
In a field of a hundred pea plants,

00:01:33.574 --> 00:01:34.613
nine are short.

00:01:35.174 --> 00:01:38.053
Short is recessive, so those nine must have

00:01:38.214 --> 00:01:39.573
two short alleles.

00:01:40.054 --> 00:01:42.613
That's a genotype frequency of nine per cent.

00:01:43.254 --> 00:01:45.093
But a tall plant could be hiding a

00:01:45.174 --> 00:01:48.053
short allele, or not, and both kinds look

00:01:48.134 --> 00:01:48.693
the same.

00:01:49.174 --> 00:01:51.973
So looks alone can't count every allele.

00:01:52.374 --> 00:01:54.453
For that, you have to read the DNA

00:01:54.574 --> 00:01:55.253
itself.

00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:58.546
Line up the same stretch of DNA from

00:01:58.707 --> 00:02:01.106
several people, and most bases match.

00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:04.066
But at some positions, people differ by just

00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:05.106
one base.

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:08.546
Here, some copies have C, and others have

00:02:08.627 --> 00:02:09.106
T.

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:13.506
That's a single nucleotide polymorphism, or snip for

00:02:13.587 --> 00:02:13.986
short.

00:02:14.707 --> 00:02:18.706
Single nucleotide means one base, and polymorphism means

00:02:18.787 --> 00:02:20.786
more than one form in the population.

00:02:21.347 --> 00:02:22.946
Each form is an allele.

00:02:23.427 --> 00:02:26.306
And because both copies are read, every genotype

00:02:26.387 --> 00:02:28.786
shows directly, carriers included.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.040
Two unrelated people differ at roughly one base

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.000
in every thousand, so each of us carries

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.760
millions of snips.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.000
Most sit in DNA that doesn't code for

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.120
a protein, and change nothing we can see.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.880
A few do matter.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.040
The sickle cell allele from the proteins video

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.480
is one: a single A changed to T.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.800
So snips are markers we can count, and

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.640
only sometimes causes.

00:02:55.867 --> 00:02:58.146
Now count them, just like the cattle.

00:02:58.427 --> 00:03:00.746
Fifty people are tested at this snip.

00:03:00.827 --> 00:03:04.346
Eighteen have C on both copies, twenty-four have

00:03:04.427 --> 00:03:07.066
one C and one T, and eight have

00:03:07.227 --> 00:03:08.666
T on both.

00:03:08.707 --> 00:03:11.306
Fifty people carry a hundred copies.

00:03:11.387 --> 00:03:14.186
The T alleles come to twenty-four, plus sixteen,

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:15.626
which makes forty.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:18.026
So the T allele frequency is forty per

00:03:18.187 --> 00:03:18.906
cent.

00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:21.146
Count alleles, not people.

00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:25.246
Snips become evidence when you compare groups.

00:03:25.807 --> 00:03:28.126
Say those fifty people all have a particular

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:31.086
disease, and fifty people without it are tested

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:31.566
too.

00:03:32.287 --> 00:03:34.766
In that second group, T is only twenty

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:35.406
per cent.

00:03:35.887 --> 00:03:38.766
That's a trend: T turns up more often

00:03:38.847 --> 00:03:39.886
with the disease.

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.206
We call that an association.

00:03:42.687 --> 00:03:45.166
But it doesn't prove that T causes it.

00:03:45.647 --> 00:03:47.446
The snip might just sit close to the

00:03:47.487 --> 00:03:50.526
DNA that really matters, on the same chromosome.

00:03:51.087 --> 00:03:54.206
Close neighbours are rarely split by crossing over,

00:03:54.287 --> 00:03:56.046
so they're inherited together.

00:03:57.267 --> 00:03:59.746
And one snip is only one marker.

00:04:00.227 --> 00:04:02.546
Two populations can share a version at one

00:04:02.627 --> 00:04:05.986
snip, and still differ at thousands of others.

00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:09.266
That's why studies of relatedness and ancestry compare

00:04:09.347 --> 00:04:11.266
many snips at once, and look at the

00:04:11.507 --> 00:04:12.546
overall pattern.

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:15.766
Every one of these numbers comes from a

00:04:15.847 --> 00:04:18.566
sample, and samples have limits.

00:04:18.887 --> 00:04:21.046
Imagine a whole breed where the white allele

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:23.046
really is twenty per cent.

00:04:23.127 --> 00:04:25.286
Sample twenty cattle, and you might get ten

00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:27.206
per cent, or thirty.

00:04:27.287 --> 00:04:29.686
Sample two hundred, and you'll stay close to

00:04:29.767 --> 00:04:30.726
twenty.

00:04:30.807 --> 00:04:33.086
Small samples bounce around.

00:04:33.447 --> 00:04:36.646
A sample also has to represent the population:

00:04:36.727 --> 00:04:39.526
one farm, or one family, may not.

00:04:39.847 --> 00:04:42.726
And everyone has to sort the same way.

00:04:42.767 --> 00:04:44.326
Is a red cow with a few white

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.166
hairs red, or roan?

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:51.926
Three words to take away: frequency, snip, limits.

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:55.286
Frequency is a count over a total, so

00:04:55.527 --> 00:04:56.766
say what you counted.

00:04:57.047 --> 00:04:59.366
A snip is a one-base difference that you

00:04:59.447 --> 00:05:00.966
can count in DNA.

00:05:01.207 --> 00:05:03.686
And limits remind you what a sample can't

00:05:03.767 --> 00:05:04.326
prove.

00:05:05.534 --> 00:05:07.133
Two harder questions now.

00:05:07.694 --> 00:05:10.973
First, a class samples white clover plants at

00:05:11.054 --> 00:05:12.813
two sites on the school grounds.

00:05:13.374 --> 00:05:15.373
Some plants have a pale V mark on

00:05:15.454 --> 00:05:16.973
their leaves, and some don't.

00:05:17.534 --> 00:05:20.173
At site A, eighteen plants have the mark

00:05:20.334 --> 00:05:21.773
and forty-two don't.

00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:24.733
At site B, thirty have the mark and

00:05:24.894 --> 00:05:25.613
thirty don't.

00:05:26.334 --> 00:05:28.173
State the frequency of the leaf mark at

00:05:28.254 --> 00:05:31.693
each site, compare the sites, and identify one

00:05:31.774 --> 00:05:33.133
limitation of the data.

00:05:33.694 --> 00:05:34.973
Pause here to answer.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.320
Each site has sixty plants.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.680
Site A is eighteen out of sixty, or

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.200
thirty per cent.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:54.920
Site B is thirty out of sixty, or

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.200
fifty per cent.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.280
So the mark is more common in site

00:05:58.440 --> 00:05:59.520
B's sample.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.040
But sixty plants from one school is a

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.000
small sample, and the mark ranges from bold

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.640
to very faint, so people can sort plants

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:08.480
differently.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.720
If you divided eighteen by forty-two, you used

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:12.840
the wrong total.

00:06:14.067 --> 00:06:14.546
Second.

00:06:15.027 --> 00:06:17.426
Forty people are tested at a snip, where

00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:19.666
the base is either G or A.

00:06:19.987 --> 00:06:23.266
Twenty have G on both copies, sixteen have

00:06:23.347 --> 00:06:25.906
one G and one A, and four have

00:06:25.987 --> 00:06:27.026
A on both.

00:06:27.347 --> 00:06:30.386
Calculate the frequency of the A allele.

00:06:30.547 --> 00:06:33.106
Then, a study finds that A is more

00:06:33.227 --> 00:06:35.586
common in people with a certain disease.

00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:38.386
Does this show that A causes the disease?

00:06:38.507 --> 00:06:39.506
Explain.

00:06:39.667 --> 00:06:40.866
Pause here to answer.

00:06:51.267 --> 00:06:53.746
Forty people carry eighty copies.

00:06:53.987 --> 00:06:56.306
The A alleles are sixteen from the mixed

00:06:56.387 --> 00:06:59.346
genotypes, plus eight from the four people with

00:06:59.467 --> 00:07:00.706
A on both copies.

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:03.986
That's twenty-four out of eighty, or thirty per

00:07:04.147 --> 00:07:04.786
cent.

00:07:04.867 --> 00:07:07.666
And no, it's an association, not proof.

00:07:08.147 --> 00:07:10.866
A might just be inherited alongside the DNA

00:07:10.947 --> 00:07:12.226
that causes the disease.

00:07:12.867 --> 00:07:14.786
If you said ten per cent, you counted

00:07:15.027 --> 00:07:16.626
people, not alleles.

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:21.513
So, with frequency data: say what you counted,

00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:25.593
count alleles rather than individuals, read the trend,

00:07:25.674 --> 00:07:27.273
then name the limits.

00:07:27.514 --> 00:07:30.153
And an association is a pattern, not a

00:07:30.234 --> 00:07:30.793
cause.

00:07:31.674 --> 00:07:34.793
Next, you'll collect and present frequency data yourself.

00:07:35.274 --> 00:07:37.913
Then we'll see how scientists read DNA, by

00:07:38.074 --> 00:07:39.913
sequencing and profiling.

00:07:41.134 --> 00:07:42.573
Here are the key notes.

00:07:42.814 --> 00:07:44.733
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:44.814 --> 00:07:45.613
to copy them.
